Chapter 9
The next day was quiet.
Raira had given the new students a day off after the introduction ceremonies, and Curtis intended to use every hour of it. He had changed into a plain black long-sleeve compression shirt and loose pants, left the braids down, and stretched out on the couch with the television murmuring in the background. The fresh stitches on his shoulder and scalp pulled whenever he shifted too quickly. The deep fatigue from blood loss, the fight, the long ride, and everything else had finally caught up.
He stared at the ceiling for a while.
“I wanna go out,” he muttered to the empty flat, “but I’m so tired today.”
The television kept talking to itself. Outside, Ikebukuro continued without him. For once he didn’t feel the immediate need to move.
His phone buzzed against his stomach.
Curtis lifted it, mismatched eyes half-lidded, and saw the name on the screen.
Rio.
He unlocked it and opened the message, the soft glow of the screen reflecting off the black fabric of his shirt as he waited to see what she wanted.
Curtis opened the message.
Rio:
Hey…
It’s Rio.
I know you said I could text if I needed to talk, or if things got heavy.
Today’s been quiet, but I keep thinking about everything.
Are you free at all?
You don’t have to say yes. I just… wanted to ask.
The typing indicator disappeared. No follow-up. She had sent exactly what she meant and then stopped, leaving the choice with him.
Curtis stared at the screen for a few seconds, the television still murmuring in the background, the stitches on his shoulder a dull reminder of the day before. The flat was quiet. His body wanted to stay on the couch.
He read the message one more time.
Curtis typed with one thumb, still stretched out on the couch.
Curtis:
I’m just sitting at home.
I’m free.
You ok?
The reply came faster than he expected.
Rio:
Yeah. I’m okay.
Better than the other night, at least.
I just… didn’t really want to sit with my thoughts by myself today.
Is it alright if I come over?
You can say no. I won’t be weird about it.
A second message followed almost immediately, like she had second-guessed the first one.
Rio:
Or we can just keep texting.
Whatever’s easier for you.
Curtis read both messages twice, the television light flickering across the black compression shirt and the long braids resting against his chest. The flat was quiet. He was tired. But the question still sat there waiting.
Curtis rolled his eyes at the ceiling and let out a long, quiet sigh.
Of course she was already giving him an exit.
Of course she was worried about being a bother.
She’s still treating herself like she’s imposing just by existing.
I told her she could text. I told her she mattered. And she’s still asking permission like I’m going to snatch it back.
He stared at the two messages a moment longer, thumb hovering over the keyboard. His body still felt heavy from the day before—stitches tight, head dull, the deep tiredness that came after losing too much blood and refusing to rest properly. Having someone in the flat meant talking. It meant being present. It meant not disappearing into the couch for the rest of the day.
…But if I say no, she’s going to sit with it alone again.
Another sigh, softer this time.
Justice would’ve already told her to come over.
Curtis’s mismatched eyes flicked toward the closed notepad on the nightstand, then back to the phone.
He started typing.
Curtis typed it out before he could overthink it.
Curtis:
Yeah if you want.
I get you live with your parents.
I live on my own.
That cool?
He sent it, dropped the phone on his chest, and stared at the ceiling again. The television kept murmuring. The stitches pulled when he shifted.
The reply came within a minute.
Rio:
Yeah. That’s cool.
Really cool, actually.
I’ll head over in a bit.
Thank you.
…For saying yes.
A final short message followed.
Rio:
I’ll text when I’m downstairs.
Curtis read the last line twice, then locked the phone and let it rest against his sternum. The flat suddenly felt a little less empty and a little more complicated at the same time.
He had about twenty minutes to decide whether to change out of the compression shirt or just accept that she was going to see him looking tired and human.
Curtis made the decision without much debate.
I really don’t care how she sees me. It’s my day off and I’m just going to nap until she gets here.
He didn’t change. He didn’t clean up the few things on the table. He simply adjusted the pillow under his head, stretched out fully on the couch in the black compression shirt and loose pants, and let his eyes fall half-closed. The long braids spilled over the cushion. The stitches on his shoulder and scalp gave a dull, familiar reminder that yesterday had happened, then settled into the background.
The television stayed on some movie channel, volume low. Dialogue and score hummed quietly through the flat, filling the space without demanding attention. Afternoon light filtered in through the half-drawn blinds. Outside, the city kept moving. Inside, for the first time in days, nothing was required of him.
Curtis’s breathing slowed.
He was asleep before Rio even left her house.
Five minutes before Rio was due, Curtis’s eyes opened.
He stretched on the couch, joints popping quietly, and rubbed a hand over his face.
“Nothing beats a power nap.”
As he sat up, thin white smoke-like tendrils slipped from his shoulders and the tips of his braids without any conscious effort. They drifted across the low table, collected the few empty cups and stray wrappers from the day before, and vanished them into nothing. By the time Curtis planted his feet on the floor, the surface was clean.
He didn’t acknowledge it. The tendrils faded as quickly as they had appeared.
Curtis yawned, long and unhurried, then pushed himself upright. The black compression shirt pulled tight across his chest and shoulders as he rolled the stitched side once and headed for the small kitchen.
“I’ll put on the kettle,” he muttered to the empty flat. “And see if she wants tea or coffee.”
The electric kettle clicked on. He leaned against the counter, still half-awake, mismatched eyes half-lidded while the water began to heat. Outside, the afternoon light had shifted. Rio would be downstairs any minute.
Curtis glanced toward the door, then back at the two mugs he had set out, and waited.
The knock came soft but clear.
Curtis pushed off the counter, crossed the short distance to the door, and opened it without checking the peephole. Afternoon light spilled in around the figure standing in the hallway.
Rio looked up—and froze for half a second.
Whatever she had expected, it clearly wasn’t the fitted black compression shirt that left very little of his build to the imagination. The long braids hung loose down his chest. The tired, unguarded version of him from the couch was still visible in his posture, but the muscle underneath the fabric was impossible to miss. Her eyes flicked once across his shoulders and chest before she caught herself and looked back up at his face, a faint flush rising.
Curtis stepped aside, mismatched eyes half-lidded but calm.
“Hey, Rio. Come in.”
Rio blinked, still slightly thrown, then managed a small, awkward nod as she stepped past him into the flat.
“…Hey.” Her voice came out quieter than usual. She glanced around the clean, simply furnished space, then back at him, clearly still adjusting to the difference between the masked night version, the uniformed school version, and this. “You, um… you look different when you’re just at home.”
She hovered near the entrance for a moment longer, bag clutched in both hands, before seeming to remember she had been invited.
Rio finally stepped fully inside and let the door close behind her. She slipped her shoes off out of habit, still holding her bag a little too tightly, and followed him a few steps into the main room.
Curtis gestured vaguely at the couch and the low table, the black compression shirt pulling across his chest with the movement.
“I wasn’t planning on doing much today. Not until this evening. So my apologies.” He glanced down at himself once, then back at her. “I can go get changed if you want.”
Rio’s eyes flicked over him again before she forced them up to his face.
“No—no, it’s fine,” she said quickly. “You don’t have to change. It’s your place. You’re… allowed to be comfortable.”
A small, awkward pause.
“You just look really different like this. That’s all.”

Inner thoughts:
He’s built like someone who fights for a living and then forgets to mention it. The hoodie and the uniform hid all of that. And he’s just standing there half-asleep offering to change like I’m the one who needs protecting from it.
He saved my life. He gave me his number. He told me I mattered. And now he’s in a tight black shirt looking like that and asking if I want him to put more clothes on.
I am not prepared for this version of him.
She tightened her grip on the bag strap and tried to keep her expression neutral.
Curtis moved back toward the kitchen without waiting for her to settle fully.
“Tea or coffee?”
Rio hovered near the couch for a second, then sat carefully on the edge of it, bag resting against her legs.
“…Tea is fine. Thank you.”
The kettle had already boiled. Curtis poured for both of them, the simple domestic motion strangely at odds with everything else she knew about him. He brought the mugs over, set one in front of her, and dropped back onto the opposite end of the couch with a quiet exhale, long braids shifting against the black compression shirt.
Rio wrapped her hands around the warm mug and stared at it for a moment before she started talking.
“I didn’t really have a big reason for coming,” she said quietly. “Nothing new happened. My parents are the same. The house is the same. I just… kept replaying the other night. And then seeing you at school. And then you giving me your number like it was nothing.”
She glanced at him, then away again.
“I thought if I stayed home today I’d just sit with it until it got heavy again. And you said I could text if I needed to talk. So I did.” A small, self-conscious breath. “I wasn’t sure you’d actually say yes.”
She took a careful sip of the tea, still not quite looking at him directly.
“You don’t have to fix anything. I just didn’t want to be alone with it for a while.”
Curtis looked at her over the rim of his mug, mismatched eyes steady.
“Why would I say no?”
A small, genuine smile pulled at the corner of his mouth—soft and unguarded in a way the masked version of him never allowed. He set the mug down on the table and leaned back against the couch, the black compression shirt shifting across his chest.
“So. I never asked in class.” His tone stayed easy. “How did you figure it was me?”
Rio’s fingers tightened slightly around her tea. She glanced at him, then down at the mug again, a faint flush still lingering from earlier.
“I saw you,” she said quietly. “Later that same night. You were with a band on the street—busking. I didn’t realize it was you at first because of the mask and the hoodie. But then the hood fell back during the second song and the braids came out.”
She gave a small, almost self-conscious shrug.
“There aren’t many people in Ikebukuro with braids like yours. Once I saw them, I knew. And then the next morning you walked into class with the same braids and the same voice… it wasn’t hard to put together.”
She finally looked at him fully again.
“I didn’t say anything because you seemed like you were giving me space. And I wasn’t sure if you even wanted me to know it was you.”
Curtis gave a small, rueful huff of amusement and rubbed the back of his neck.
“Oh yeah. I was a tired mess. I often stay out late and don’t sleep much.” He gestured vaguely at himself with the mug. “Energized by night. A zombie by day.”
Rio watched him for a moment, the tea warming her hands.
“…That explains a lot,” she said quietly. “You fell asleep in class twice on the first day. And just now you looked like you were still half-asleep when you opened the door.”
A small, almost hesitant smile tugged at her mouth.
“You’re really not good at pretending to be a normal student, are you?”
She took another sip, then added more softly,
“Doesn’t bother me. I’d rather know the real version than the one that tries to act like everyone else.”
She glanced at the dark circles under his mismatched eyes, then away again, the quiet of the flat settling comfortably between them.
Curtis tilted his head slightly, long braids shifting against the black compression shirt.
“Hmm. The real version, huh.” A small pause. “Both are me.”
Rio let out a quiet chuckle—soft and genuine, the first real one she had given since walking in. Some of the remaining tension in her shoulders eased with it.
“Yeah… I guess they are,” she said, still smiling a little. “The one who catches people off rooftops and the one who falls asleep in class and answers the door half-awake in a tight shirt.”
She glanced at him sideways, the flush from earlier mostly gone now.
“It’s just taking me a minute to fit them together. Most people only get one version. You keep showing me different ones like it’s nothing.”
She took another sip of tea, then added more quietly,
“I think I like that you don’t bother pretending they’re separate, though.”
Curtis leaned back into the couch, mug resting loosely in one hand, mismatched eyes half-lidded but attentive.
“Well. What would you like to know about me?” A small pause. “And then tell me about yourself, Rio.”
The invitation sat quietly between them—no pressure, no performance. Just an open door.
Rio looked down at her tea for a second, clearly turning the question over. When she spoke, her voice was careful but steadier than when she had first walked in.
“…Okay.” She glanced up at him. “You don’t have to answer anything you don’t want to. But… where are you actually from? You said you were a wanderer, and you speak Japanese really well, but you don’t feel like you’re from here.”
A small breath.
“And… why Ikebukuro? Out of everywhere you could’ve gone.”
She hesitated, then added more softly,
“I’ll tell you about me after. I just… I don’t know much about you beyond the rooftop and the classroom. And I’d like to.”
Curtis took a slow sip of his tea before answering, mismatched eyes steady on the far wall for a moment.
“I’m from the Northwest Territories, but I grew up in the Appalachian forest. Then I traveled around for a while before flying here from Los Angeles.” He gave a small shrug. “I studied languages for quite some time. Hence why my Japanese is good.”
A short pause.
“And Ikebukuro seemed like a nice place. I even chose the college.”
It was the cleaned-up version—true in the broad strokes, carefully missing the parts about being abandoned, the things in the forest, the scars, and everything else he had already let slip in front of Shinra and Celty. He delivered it the same way he delivered most personal information: flat, practical, and without invitation for further digging.
Rio listened quietly, both hands wrapped around her mug.
“…That’s a long way to come just because a place ‘seemed nice,’” she said after a moment. There was no accusation in it—just quiet curiosity. “Most people pick a city for school or work or family. You make it sound like you looked at a map and decided this was as good a spot as any.”
She studied him for a second longer, then offered a small, almost self-deprecating smile.
“I guess that fits the wanderer thing, though.”
A beat passed before she added, softer,
“Thank you for telling me. Even if it’s only the surface version.”
She didn’t push for the rest. Not yet.Durarara!! AU
Chapter 9 (continued)
Curtis accepted the observation with a small tilt of his head, neither confirming nor denying how much he had left out.
Rio set her mug down carefully and took a quiet breath, as if deciding she had been given enough to offer something in return.
“My turn, I guess,” she said.
She kept her eyes on the table for a moment before speaking.
“I’ve lived in Ikebukuro basically my whole life. Same house. Same parents. Same… everything. I’m not like you. I didn’t get to choose a city or a college because it ‘seemed nice.’ Raira was just the place that accepted me, and staying here was easier than trying to leave.”
A small, tight smile.
“You already know about my parents. That’s most of the heavy part. The rest is pretty ordinary—part-time job sometimes, average grades, not many close friends. I spend a lot of time online because it’s quieter than being in the house.”
She glanced at him, then away again.
“The other night was the first time I ever actually tried to do something about how trapped I felt. And then you showed up. And now I’m sitting in your apartment drinking tea like any of this is normal.”
Rio let out a short, quiet breath that was almost a laugh.
“I don’t really know what I’m doing. I just know that talking to you feels less heavy than sitting at home replaying everything by myself.”
She finally looked at him fully again, waiting to see what he would do with the honesty she had just given him.
Curtis’s expression softened.
“I’m glad, Rio.”
He stretched on the couch, long braids shifting across his shoulders, then glanced toward the window where the light had begun to turn gold.
“Evening soon.”
He stood without further explanation and disappeared into the bedroom. A few minutes later the door opened again.
Curtis stepped out in a clean white dress shirt, black suspenders, red tie, and black pants. The fitted shirt sat neatly across his frame; the suspenders and tie gave the whole look a sharp, almost host-like polish that was a far cry from the tired compression shirt she had arrived to. He adjusted one cuff absently as he walked back into the main room, mismatched eyes calm.
“As I said before,” he said simply, “I tend to wander at night.”
Rio’s gaze caught on the outfit and stayed there a second too long. A visible flush rose across her cheeks before she managed to look up at his face again.
“…You really don’t do anything halfway, do you?” she said, voice a little quieter than before. She gestured vaguely at him, still clearly adjusting to the sudden shift. “First the street version, then the school version, then the home version… and now you look like you’re about to work a high-end bar.”
A small, self-conscious breath escaped her.
“Are you going out right now? Or… is this just what night-Curtis looks like?”
Curtis glanced down at the white shirt and suspenders as if only now considering how the outfit read to someone else.
“I have two outfits for the evening. This one because I plan to drive around.” He gave a small shrug. “And my other one for when I wander through the night on foot. Mostly depends how I feel like dressing.”
Rio let out a quiet, almost disbelieving breath and shook her head, the flush still lingering on her cheeks.
“…You have designated night outfits.” She said it like she was confirming a strange but somehow fitting fact about him. “Of course you do.”
She looked at him again, more openly this time, taking in the sharp contrast between the tired man who had answered the door in a compression shirt and the one standing in front of her now.
“So this is the ‘driving around’ version.” A small pause. “Does that mean you’re heading out soon? Or are you still okay with me being here for a while longer?”
There was no pressure in the question—just a quiet check-in, like she was giving him the same easy exit he kept offering her.
Curtis smiled—small, genuine, and a little amused by her careful question.
“Hold on a moment.”
He disappeared back into the bedroom. For a few seconds there was only quiet. Then a soft, unfamiliar shift of air moved under the door, like heat haze given form. When he stepped out again, a sleek white helmet rested in his hands. Thin trails of white smoke still clung to its surface before fading completely, as if the helmet had been shaped out of the air itself rather than taken from a shelf.
Curtis held it out loosely by the chin strap.
“Do you wanna ride home?”
Rio stared.
First at the helmet that had not existed a minute ago.
Then at him.
Then back at the helmet.
“…Ride?” Her voice came out smaller than before. “I thought you had a car.”
She looked at the white helmet again, clearly still processing the way it had appeared, and then up at the sharp evening version of him standing in the white shirt and suspenders.
“You have a motorcycle.”
It wasn’t quite a question. More like the final piece of the evening-Curtis puzzle clicking into place—and bringing a new set of nerves with it.
Curtis gave a small nod, still holding the white helmet loosely at his side.
“Yeah. I actually just bought it yesterday.” A short pause, the smile still faint at the corner of his mouth. “But we’ve got some time to kill and hang out. Hungry?”
Rio shook her head.
“I’m okay. I ate before I came.”
So they stayed.
The next stretch of time passed quietly. The television stayed on low in the background. They talked in shorter, easier pieces—nothing as heavy as the rooftop or her parents, just the kind of scattered conversation that filled the flat without demanding too much from either of them. Rio gradually relaxed into the couch. Curtis remained in the white shirt and suspenders, long braids resting against his chest, the earlier tiredness softened by the company.
Eventually the light outside shifted fully into evening.
Curtis glanced at the clock, then stood.
“Time.”
He disappeared into the bedroom once more. When he came back out he had layered the gray-and-red jacket over the shirt and suspenders, settled the red Jumpman cap over his braids, and slipped on the gold-framed sunglasses. A black side bag hung across his chest. In one hand he carried his own helmet—the crimson custom one with the glowing visor—and in the other he still held the white one he had formed for her.
He stopped in the main room, ready.
Rio looked up at the full evening version of him—jacket, cap, sunglasses, helmets, bag—and went quiet for a second.
“…You really do transform when the sun goes down,” she said softly. Her eyes moved from the sharp outfit to the two helmets, then back to his face. “I’m a little nervous. I’ve never been on a motorcycle before.”
She stood anyway, brushing her hands against her skirt, and managed a small, determined breath.
“But… okay. I’m ready if you are.”
Curtis paused at the door, both helmets in hand, and gave her one last clear out.
“I don’t want to force you. Are you sure?”
Rio nodded, more firmly this time.
“I’m sure.”

The red Softail waited in the garage like it had been patient all day. Curtis secured the black side bag, handed Rio the white helmet, and helped her with the strap when her fingers fumbled. Once she was settled behind him—arms loosely around his waist, the gray-and-red jacket under her hands—he started the engine. The deep, rolling growl filled the underground space, then echoed out onto the street as they pulled away.
Ikebukuro’s evening lights slid past them in streaks of neon and headlights.

Rio’s inner thoughts:
This bike is… really nice.
It doesn’t feel like the loud, rattling ones I’ve seen around the station. It feels heavy and smooth at the same time, like it was built to eat distance.
And he just bought it yesterday. With cash. Like it was nothing.
I’m on the back of a motorcycle with the same person who caught me off a rooftop, and somehow this feels safer than half the conversations I have at home.
Don’t grip too tight. Don’t make it weird. Just… breathe.
She adjusted her hold slightly against his back, the white helmet visor reflecting the red of the bike and the glow of the city as Curtis took a clean, unhurried route through the streets.
Rio kept her arms loosely around his waist as the red Softail carried them through the evening streets, the white helmet he had given her turning the city into a slightly muffled, closer world.
Her thoughts drifted, quiet and circling.
Even the helmet covers everything.
The glowing visor, the way it seals over his whole face… it’s just another version of the mask. School uniform, compression shirt, this sharp jacket and tie—he’ll change the clothes, but he still won’t let anyone see all of him at once.
He bought a helmet that looks more expensive than most people’s rent, and the first thing it does is hide him again.
A different thought followed, softer and more confused.
I’ve never seen him in short sleeves either.
Not the night he caught me, not at school, not today at his place. Always long sleeves. Always covered. Even when he was half-asleep on his own couch he kept everything hidden.
Is he just always cold? Or is there something else?
She didn’t ask.
She simply adjusted her grip slightly against the gray-and-red jacket, the vibration of the engine steady under them, and watched the neon slide past the edges of the white visor while the rider in front of her remained completely sealed behind red light and black shell.
Curtis brought the red Softail to a smooth stop a short distance from her building—close enough to be convenient, far enough that the loud engine wouldn’t draw unnecessary attention from her parents’ windows. He killed the ignition and planted his feet, the glowing red visor turning toward her as she carefully climbed off.
Rio unclipped the white helmet with slightly unsteady fingers and held it out to him.
“…Thank you,” she said, voice quiet but clear in the sudden absence of the engine. “For the ride. And for letting me come over today. And for… not making any of it feel like I was bothering you.”
She glanced at the black-and-red helmet still sealing his face, then down at the white one in her hands before he took it back.
“You’re kind of hard to figure out, Curtis. But I’m glad you said yes.”
A small, genuine smile pulled at her mouth—tired, a little shy, but real.
“Text me when you get home? Or… whenever. I’ll answer.”
She lingered on the sidewalk for a second longer, as if not quite ready to step back into the version of her life that waited upstairs, then finally turned toward the building.
Curtis’s voice came through the helmet’s speakers—smooth, slightly filtered, cooler than his bare tone.
“Tonight I think I’ll ride around the area and get used to it.”
Rio paused on the first step up to her building and looked back at him. The red Softail sat low and quiet now, the glowing circular visor fixed on her, the modular red braids hanging still against the gray-and-red jacket. Even with the voice modulation, it still sounded like him—just more distant, more sealed away.
“…Okay,” she said softly. “Be careful.”
A small beat passed. She shifted her weight, then added,
“And… thank you again. For today. It helped more than I expected.”
She gave him one last small smile, then turned and headed up the stairs toward the entrance. At the door she glanced back once more, as if checking that the red motorcycle and the rider in the glowing helmet were still there, before disappearing inside.
Curtis remained where he was for a moment longer, engine still off, the filtered quiet of the helmet settling around him again. Then he started the Softail, the deep growl rolling down the quiet residential street, and pulled away into the night.
